CAT = "inbdeEthics"; SEG = "ETH"
# FK9: behavioral sciences, ethics and jurisprudence (ADA Code, HIPAA, OSHA, patient management).
Q = [
("A general dentist sends a patient with a calcified, curved molar canal to an endodontist rather than attempt it. Under the ADA Code, this mainly reflects:",
 "Nonmaleficence", ["Patient autonomy", "Justice", "Veracity"],
 "The ADA Code places consultation and referral under nonmaleficence (do no harm): dentists seek help from those with special skills when it protects the patient."),
("When an endodontist finishes treating a referred patient, what does the ADA Code expect, unless the patient asks otherwise?",
 "Return the patient to the referrer", ["Keep the patient for all future care", "Send the patient to a new general dentist", "Discharge the patient with no referral"],
 "Under the Code, specialists and consultants return the patient to the referring dentist, or to the dentist of record, unless the patient expressly prefers otherwise."),
("A dentist accepts an insurer's payment as payment in full and quietly never collects the patient's copayment. Under the ADA Code, this is:",
 "Overbilling the insurer", ["An act of beneficence", "Acceptable if the patient is poor", "A private fee arrangement"],
 "The Code calls this overbilling: the claim makes the charge look higher than it really is, which is deception under the principle of veracity, unless the waiver is disclosed to the third party."),
("A dentist stops treating a patient halfway through a bridge after a dispute, without notice. Under the ADA Code, this is:",
 "Patient abandonment", ["Justifiable criticism", "Informed refusal", "Lawful discharge"],
 "Once treatment has begun, the dentist should not stop without adequate notice and a chance for the patient to find another dentist, and the patient's oral health must not be put at risk."),
("A dentist knows first-hand that a colleague is treating patients while impaired by alcohol. Under the ADA Code, what is expected?",
 "Urge treatment and report the evidence", ["Say nothing unless a patient complains", "Report only after a second incident occurs", "Report him to the police at once"],
 "The Code obliges dentists to urge impaired colleagues to seek treatment and to report first-hand evidence to the professional assistance committee of a dental society."),
("A dentist recommends removing sound amalgam fillings from a non-allergic patient to rid the body of toxins. Under the ADA Code, this is:",
 "Unethical, violating veracity", ["Acceptable if the patient agrees", "Required by beneficence", "A matter of patient autonomy alone"],
 "The Code states that removing amalgam from a non-allergic patient for the alleged purpose of removing toxins, on the dentist's own recommendation, is improper and unethical under veracity."),
("A new patient's existing restorations are poorly done. Under the ADA Code's view of justifiable criticism, what should the dentist do?",
 "State the facts without disparaging", ["Tell the patient the last dentist was negligent", "Say nothing about the restorations", "Report the last dentist to the board at once"],
 "Patients should be told the facts of their current oral health without disparaging comment about prior work, and consulting the previous dentist is encouraged; only gross or continual faulty treatment is reported."),
("Which element must be part of valid informed consent for an extraction?",
 "Risks, benefits and alternatives", ["The fee schedule of the office", "The dentist's years in practice", "A signed waiver of liability"],
 "Informed consent means the patient understands the diagnosis, the proposed treatment, its risks and benefits and the reasonable alternatives, including no treatment."),
("A dental practice bills insurers electronically. A billing company processes those claims for it. Under HIPAA, the billing company is a:",
 "Business associate", ["Covered entity", "Health plan", "Personal representative"],
 "A business associate performs functions involving protected health information on behalf of a covered entity and must sign a business associate agreement; the practice that transmits claims electronically is the covered entity."),
("Under HIPAA, a patient who asks for a copy of her own record must generally receive it within:",
 "30 days of the request", ["7 days of the request", "90 days of the request", "One year of the request"],
 "The right of access requires action within 30 days of the request, with one 30-day extension allowed if the patient is told why in writing."),
("A laptop with unencrypted records of 120 patients is stolen. Under the HIPAA Breach Notification Rule, the patients must be told:",
 "Within 60 days of discovery", ["Within 24 hours of discovery", "Only if the press reports it", "Only after one year passes"],
 "Individuals must be notified without unreasonable delay and no later than 60 days after discovery. Breaches of fewer than 500 people are also logged and reported to HHS each year."),
("A patient's benefits year has just reset, and she asks the dentist to date last month's crown as this month so the plan pays. Under the ADA Code, doing so is:",
 "A false representation to the plan", ["Acceptable for a loyal patient", "Allowed if the fee is unchanged", "Allowed once per benefits year"],
 "Reporting incorrect treatment dates to help a patient get benefits that would otherwise be disallowed is an unethical, false or misleading representation under the Code's veracity section."),
("A dentist praises a 5-year-old at once each time she keeps her mouth open. Which behavior principle is being used?",
 "Positive reinforcement", ["Negative reinforcement", "Positive punishment", "Extinction"],
 "Adding something pleasant right after a behavior makes it more likely to happen again. Negative reinforcement removes something unpleasant, and extinction withholds reinforcement."),
("A dentist helps a fearful adult relax while gradually facing more anxiety-provoking steps, from sitting in the chair to an injection. This is:",
 "Systematic desensitization", ["Aversive conditioning", "Positive punishment", "Flooding the patient"],
 "Systematic desensitization pairs relaxation with a graded hierarchy of feared stimuli, so each step is tolerated before moving on."),
("Under Piaget's stages, a typical 4-year-old who thinks the drill is angry at him is in which stage?",
 "Preoperational", ["Sensorimotor", "Concrete operational", "Formal operational"],
 "Children of about 2 to 7 years are preoperational: egocentric, magical thinking and giving life to objects, so simple, concrete words work best."),
("A Spanish-speaking patient brings her 10-year-old son to interpret for a discussion of oral surgery. What is best?",
 "Use a trained medical interpreter", ["Let the son interpret everything", "Speak more slowly in English", "Ask the front-desk clerk to translate"],
 "Children should not interpret clinical information: they may lack vocabulary, omit details and face a burden. A trained interpreter supports valid informed consent."),
("Case: 81-year-old woman with moderate Alzheimer's disease needs extractions. Her daughter holds her durable power of attorney for health care. Who gives consent?",
 "The daughter, as her legal representative", ["The patient alone, because she is present", "The dentist, acting in her best interest", "Any family member who comes along"],
 "When a patient lacks capacity, the person legally authorized, here the health care agent, gives consent, while the patient is still involved as far as she is able."),
("Case: 34-year-old man with HIV, well controlled on therapy, asks for routine fillings. The dentist feels uneasy. Under the ADA Code, what is required?",
 "Treat him as any other patient", ["Refer him to an HIV dental clinic", "Treat him only at day's end", "Ask for his viral load in writing"],
 "The Code holds that a dentist may not refuse a patient because of a bloodborne infection; decisions on treatment or referral are made on the same basis as for any patient, and standard precautions apply to all."),
("Case: 7-year-old girl with untreated decay in many teeth and a facial abscess; the parent has missed several appointments despite reminders and free transport. What is the dentist's duty?",
 "Report suspected neglect", ["Wait for the parent to call back", "Report it if she misses one more visit", "Close the chart and discharge her"],
 "Willful failure to get needed care for a child, after the parent understands the problem and barriers have been addressed, can be dental neglect, and the ADA Code obliges dentists to report suspected neglect under state law."),
("Case: A patient calls on Saturday with severe pain and swelling after a root canal begun two days ago. Under the ADA Code, the dentist must:",
 "Arrange reasonable emergency care", ["Tell him to wait until Monday", "Refer him to an endodontist next week", "Charge first, then decide to help"],
 "Dentists are obliged to make reasonable arrangements for emergency care of their patients of record, whether by seeing them or arranging coverage."),
]
